Z||000004||FieroJoe||12-06-2001||12:57 PM||||Looks great!  I am glad that our hoist thingy worked out there.<P>For those of you who don't know what I am talking about, you can see in the fourth pic two large metal poles bolted to the ground.  I think they were basketball hoop poles.  Anyways, up on top of them we bolted a 2x2" square metal tube all the way across, secured to each pole.  Stan then hung a chainfall from that and hoisted his car that way.  Cheaper than a engine hoist because he already had all the stuff!<P>The 3800 he has is very nice too, it has only 11 miles on it.  Not eleven hundred, not eleven thousand, but only ELEVEN!  Brand new!<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||12-06-2001||02:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>...you can see in the fourth pic two large metal poles bolted to the ground.  I think they were basketball hoop poles.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They look like those steel poles that are used to support floor joists in houses with a basement. <BR>Wish I'd have though about that before I went out and bought a hoist.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>Lookin' good!<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
